:: Day 3 EV

The ledger lay on the ferry-house table between the three of them, and the stove ticked, and nobody had slept.

Tam read it the way she read weather, without moving her lips. When she came to the last page she put her finger on her own name and left it there.

"Thirteen people have drowned at that weir in my time," she said. "I can name every one. None of them were in a book first."

"The dates are eleven days off," Wren said.

"Eleven days." Tam closed the book. "The wardens have wanted this crossing since before the captain was born. This is just the first time they've written it down." She looked at Wren, and then at Pip, and then at the brass lantern on its hook by the door. "Brother Ilex will know whose ink that is. He knows every hand on the hill. Or we put it in the stove and let them come and ask for it."

Wren looked at the lantern too, and at the bell tower on the hill, grey in the first light.

[[Take the ledger up to Brother Ilex->Gull Chapel at dusk]]
[[Put it in the stove->Day 4 EV]]

:: The dark crossing

They crossed an hour after the bell, Tam at the stern with the ash pole and Wren at the bow with nothing in her hands at all. The lantern stayed on its hook in the ferry house. Wren had never crossed without it, and she found the dark on the water was a different dark from the dark on land: it had no edges.

"A hundred nights I've crossed like this," Tam said behind her, low. "More. The lantern's a habit, girl. It was never a rule."

Halfway over, a light showed downstream, green, bobbing. The wardens' boat. Tam stopped poling and let the current hold them, and the light went on past and did not turn.

"Told you. Drunk at the Reach."

The far jetty came up under the bow, and Pip was on it with a key in his fist, and behind him the old mill stood black against a sky only a little less black.

"The mill," he said. "Now. Before they come back."

[[Day 3 EV]]

:: The warden's ledger

Wren opened the book.

The pages were ruled in the wardens' hand, columns for name and toll and date, and the entries ran back years. Tamsin Reeve, month paid on the first, every month for as far back as the pages went. And then, on the last written page, three lines in a different ink: Tamsin Reeve. Wren Halloway. Pip Halloway. The dates beside them were eleven days off.

"It's a toll book," Wren said. "It's just the toll book."

"Then why is it here?" Pip asked. "And why are we in it?"

The poling outside had stopped. Captain Marsh stood in the doorway with the light behind him, and he did not look surprised to see either of them. Marsh looked at Pip, and he said nothing for a long moment.

"That book is wardens' property," Marsh said at last. "So is this house. So, as of eleven days from now, is the ferry. Old Tam's had forty years of it. That's enough for anyone."

Wren closed the ledger and put it under her arm, and the captain, for reasons she would not understand for a long time, stood aside and let her carry it out.

[[Day 3 EV]]

:: The weir

Wren went out. The boat was the wardens' long boat, but there was only one man in it, and it was Captain Marsh, alone, poling against the pull of the weir with his hat gone and his coat black with spray.

"Get back in the house," he shouted.

It was the second morning since Wren had left Saltmarrow, and she had not yet learned to do what wardens told her. She went down to the water's edge. The pole in Marsh's hands caught on something under the surface and would not come free, and the boat swung broadside to the weir, and the weir took it.

She saw him go over. She saw the green pennant go over after him. She stood in the roar of it and counted, the way Tam had taught her to count a drowning, and when she reached two hundred she stopped, because nobody came up from the weir at Hollin Reach, not in forty years, not ever.

Pip was at her elbow. He had the ledger under his arm.

"We should go," he said. "Wren. We should go now."

[[The toll]]
